ns, enterprise, consumer, automotive, healthcare, industrial, and power industries in the Americas, Asia, and Europe. The company operates through three segments: Integrated Technology Solutions (ITS), Regulated Manufacturing Solutions (RMS), and Cloud and Power Infrastructure (CPI). The ITS segment offers flexible supply and manufacturing solutions for communications, including high speed networking, enterprise, and satellite communications systems, as well as lifestyle solutions comprising products across commercial, home, and personal product categories. Its RMS segment includes industrial products, such as mission critical automation, energy, and industrial infrastructure; automotive products, including compute and power electronics platforms and integrated systems; and healthcare products comprising regulated manufacturing for medical devices, drug delivery, and equipment. The CPI segment provides cloud and cooling products, such as integrated compute systems supporting power dense digital infrastructure deployments and advanced liquid cooling solutions supporting higher density, power intensive rack architectures; and power products that include utility and facility level electrical infrastructure enabling power delivery and high density rack and board level power systems. The company was formerly known as Flextronics International Ltd. and changed its name to Flex Ltd. in September 2016. Flex Ltd. was founded in 1969 and is headquartered in Austin, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Revathi  Advaithi | CEO & Director | Age 57 | Compensation: $5.9M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 149K people. Revenue per employee of $186K ranks in the bottom quartile among Technology peers. Each employee generates $7K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $949 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 32\u00d7 revenue per employee (elevated relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Flex Ltd. is a large-cap technology business with a $25.8B market cap on $27.9B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -3% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 16% against a 10% cost of capital, a 6-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it, and 6% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $1.1B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 4 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.91\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.7\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 6.4\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 3.7% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.2\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $5.1M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-5058326.61},{"text":"Gross margin trails most Technology peers \u2014 competitors appear to have the pricing hand","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Technology peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Technology sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 9% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Technology ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Elevated sector volatility (52% annualized) adds uncertainty","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cybersecurity liability and talent cost inflation \u2014 a single breach can erase years of goodwill, and the war for engineering talent compresses margins even at scale.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Reinvestment rate supports 16% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business","Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Flex (FLEX) Down 27.8% Since Last Earnings Report: Can It Rebound?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3036675/flex-flex-down-27-8-since-last-earnings-report-can-it-rebound?cid=CS-ZC-FT-realtime_blog-3036675","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795793425.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Is Flex (FLEX) Undervalued After Its CPI Growth Forecast And Rare Five Year Run?","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/tech/nasdaq-flex/flex/news/is-flex-flex-undervalued-after-its-cpi-growth-forecast-and-r","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795792509.353,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.898,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["guidance"]},{"title":"Price-Driven Insight from (FLEX) for Rule-Based Strategy","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/34/Price-Driven_Insight_from_FLEX_for_Rule-Based_Strategy_112526055402_1795647242.html","date":"2026-11-25","timestamp":1795647242.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":